              "code": "CAC-1",
              "confidence": "high",
              "detail": "Cache reads and writes are 99% of input spend across 286 priced requests. That share on its own says nothing about whether caching is working -- reads bill at a tenth of the input rate, so they dominate a bill only when the volume behind them is large. The counterfactual is what settles it: the same traffic sent uncached would cost 1.0x what it does now, so caching is adding 5% to input spend. Of the cache spend itself, 2% is reads. A session that extends its prefix rather than rebuilding it tends to run near 75%, so a lower share is worth looking into -- but it is a hint and not a finding, because a workload whose turns add a lot relative to the prefix (large tool results) moves toward even without rebuilding anything. REB-1 counts rebuilds directly and is what settles it.",
              "evidence_class": "measured",
              "fix": "Stop writing caches that nothing reads before tuning anything else.",
              "quality_risk": "low",
              "severity": "high",
